    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**This is an exceptional, top-tier offer for a Senior Product Manager (L6) at Google.**\n\nTo put it in perspective:\n* **Market Band:** The median Total Compensation (TC) for an L6 PM in the Bay Area typically sits between **$500,000 and $580,000**. Your annualized offer sits well above the 75th/90th percentile.\n* **Base Salary ($260,000):** Sits at the **88th percentile** of the posted salary range ($183,000 \u2013 $271,000).\n* **RSU Grant ($1,340,000):** Averaging $335,000/year, this is high for L6 (standard grants are typically $700k\u2013$950k over 4 years; $1.3M+ is closer to L7 / Principal PM territory).\n* **Sign-on Bonus ($200,000):** Most L6 sign-on bonuses range from $50,000 to $100,000. Google offered $200,000 specifically to make you whole on your $200,000 in unvested Meta RSUs upfront in cash on day one.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Side-by-Side Compensation Comparison\n\nGoogle uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for initial RSU grants (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**) rather than Meta\u2019s flat 25%/year:\n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Google Offer (Year 1) | Google Offer (Year 2) | Google Offer (4-Yr Avg) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $52,000 (20%) | $52,000 (20%) | $52,000 |\n| **RSUs (Annual)** | $250,000 | ~$442,200 (33% vest) | ~$442,200 (33% vest) | $335,000 |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | \u2014 | $200,000 | \u2014 | $50,000 (amortized) |\n| **Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **~$954,200** | **~$754,200** | **~$697,000/yr** |\n\n* **Year 1:** You will earn nearly **double** your Meta compensation due to the front-loaded stock and cash sign-on.\n* **4-Year Average:** You will average **+$171,000/year (+32.5%)** over your current Meta package, even without factoring in stock appreciation or annual performance refreshes.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n* **Walk Away?** **No.** The offer strongly outperforms your current compensation, and Google is your top choice.\n* **Negotiate?** **Yes, but with a targeted \"Closing Ask.\"** \n\nBecause you do not have competing offers, Google already pushed near the top of their L6 band. You should **not** negotiate aggressively or give ultimatums. However, making a single, polite, closing ask carries virtually zero risk of an offer being rescinded and can lock in extra value.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\nWhen negotiating near top-of-band without leverage, target **Base Salary** and **RSUs**:\n\n1. **Base Salary (Ask: $270,000):** \n   * *Rationale:* The public band caps at $271,000. Asking to round up to $270,000 is an easy adjustment for HR that permanently lifts your baseline for future raises and bonuses.\n2. **Equity (Ask: $1,450,000 over 4 years):**\n   * *Rationale:* Equity bands are more flexible than cash bands. Moving from $1.34M to $1.45M adds ~$36k/yr in Year 1 and Year 2.\n\n#### Negotiation Script / Email Draft\n\n> *\"Hi [Recruiter Name],*\n>\n> *Thank you again for putting this offer together. I\u2019m thrilled about the team, the scope of the L6 role, and the opportunity to join Google\u2014it remains my top choice.*\n>\n> *I took time over the weekend to review the full details and compare them against my trajectory and compensation at Meta. The offer is very strong and reflects market value well.*\n>\n> *If we can make two slight adjustments to close the loop\u2014bringing the **base salary to $270,000** (to reach the top of the stated band) and increasing the **RSU grant to $1,450,000**\u2014I am ready to sign the offer immediately today.*\n>\n> *Regardless, I appreciate all your partnership throughout this process and look forward to hearing your thoughts.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Key Things to Keep in Mind\n\n1. **The Year 3 / Year 4 Cliff:** Because Google front-loads initial equity (66% vests in the first two years), your unrefreshed equity drops significantly in Years 3 and 4. Google manages this with annual performance refreshes (which vest over 4 years at 25%/year). To maintain a $700k+ run rate in Year 3, you will rely on strong performance reviews and refresher stacking.\n2. **Tax Withholding on Year 1 Windfall:** With ~$950k+ in Year 1 income, standard supplemental wage tax withholding (22% federal) on RSUs and bonuses will fall short of your true ~37% top federal marginal rate. Set aside cash from your $200k sign-on bonus for estimated taxes.",
